<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duncansby Head</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Duncansby Head is the most north-easterly point of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, a headland near John o' Groats in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. Its sandstone cliffs, topped by a lighthouse, look over the Pentland Firth towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>. Just south of it stand the Duncansby Stacks, tall rock pillars that are among the best-known coastal landmarks of northern Scotland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/duncansby-head.jpg" alt="Duncansby Head" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Russ McLean at English Wikipedia · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duncansby_Head_lighthouse.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
